  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Combatting illegal logging in Korean broadleaf forests in the Russian Far East to conserve the.habitat of the Amur tiger</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The purpose of this project is to, (1) Develop response teams and build their capacity to ground-truth alerts from the KEDR alarm system and enforce the law against illegal loggers in Amur tiger habitats, (2) Improve digital information sharing on the quality of Amur tiger habitat and forest management in Korean-pine broadleaf forests, and, and, (3) Assist with controlling illegal logging in Amur tiger habitats. Well trained and equipped mobile response teams will be established in Khabarovsk and Primorsky provinces. Project will support the development of a database on forest concessions, forest companies, and exporters of Mongolian oak, provide weekly updates on forest cover change, and make available information on forest concessions and maps of High Conservation Value Forests on an open access data portal; Monitor KEDR implementation and efficiency of law enforcement; Cross check information from KEDR alerts, which uses the GLAD (Global Land Analysis and Discovery) algorithm alert system to alert authorities to forest cover change using medium resolution and open access (10-15 pixel) multispectral space images from Landsat and Santinel satellites. KEDR aggregates GLAD alert data, compares it with the existing forest management database, and a special control center under the administration of the Forest Department informs relevant authorities on possible unauthorized activities in the forest.</narrative>
  </description>
